Z||000002||terryk||03-08-2000||07:36 PM||||Did they say why it was running rich? A scanner is a must in this situation. I believe the ECM support CE lamp O2 monitoring. The procedure is in the manual. THe CE lamp will flicker according to the O2's voltage. <P>If it's *really* rich, its' because something is telling the O2 it lean and the ECM is adding fuel. Too much fuel. A catch 22 to a point. <P>You could have an exhaust leak or the O2 could be cooling off but that is more common at idle. Cruising it tends to keep it hot. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||william_thorniii||03-08-2000||08:34 PM||||WI GT,<P>I am currently running with a Cherry Bomb in the place of my kitty.  Clogging a muffler is almost impossible.  So, if a clogged cat is the problems that you are experiencing then the answer is yes.  <P>The pinging problem is that most people do not know how to properly set their timing to factory specifications.  Even with 104+ Octane booster added to premium fuel, it is easy to make your car ping by having the plugs fire at the wrong time.  This would also explain why your 2.8 is sluggish.  <P>You said that you have done lots of work to your car.  Have you by any chance recently rebuilt the engine.  Most engines are not fully broken in until around 50K.  Often a measureable performance increase is noticed after an engine is fully broken in.  <P>Those are all just stabs in the dark though.  Your best bet on engine diagnostics would be to head out and drop (a large chunk of) money on an OTC Monitor.  It is the same thing that most shops use to read codes.  It will be able to tell you more than you want or need to know.  <P>William T. Thorn, III<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000004||kevin||03-09-2000||01:47 AM||||I like your idea on a fiero cd rom. What I think would be cool would be to highlight for the viewers unique qualities of each year followed by what each owner has concocted to overcome the deficienys from the factory. In other words, suppose you interview a 85 SE owner. You video-tape his car doing a "live" one-on-one cross examination as you circle the inside and outside. Along the way the owner will guide the viewers and interviewer as to what he did to make his car fit his personality and how he went about to obtain the end result. This can be done with hundreds of cars, perhaps shared with the many clubs throughout the country. Each club president, or his designee can do the interview in the fassion described above. After awhile the competition will arise between the various clubs over who obtained the best, funniest, or comprhensive in depth analysis. I think that done properly and with proper editing, to keep out the drunks and knuckleheads, you would have a product that people would pay for. Let me know what you think.   ||||reg||1
